\f0\fs24 \cf0 For this case study I was inspired by the work of shigeru ban, specifically his structures that focused on using modular builds using paper rolls.\
\
The sustainability of his work really touched me and inspired me. I loved the fact that although they are cardboard, since they are build modularly, they are easy to sustain, when one piece begins to fail the material is easy to access and install. For this reason, although many of the structures he\'92s made like this are for emergency situations and they aren\'92t meant to last permanently, they can stay standing for as long as they are maintained.\
\
With this inspiration I wanted to focus on a project about ban\'92s \'91paper palaces\'92 that would also embody the principles behind them.\
\
I created a poster, that would fold into a booklet, and have tearable tabs or cards that would not destroy the integrity or design of the two prior pieces.\
\
The material for this project would be newsprint paper, since it\'92s the most affordable medium, easily accessible world-wide and also the easiest to recycle. This would be my first step to meeting the sustainability standards I set for myself in this study.\
\
The poster itself would be 24inx36in, chosen because it\'92s a common type of newsprint sheet that can be purchased in bulk, but also, can easily printed en-mass from a 24in or 36in roll of newsprint.\
\
The booklet would be printed on the opposite side of the newsprint to begin meeting the need for modularity I set for myself. This product can now be folded into a booklet and then opened and placed up as a poster.\
\
The booklet would also house tearable cards on the last page, that would organically live and work with the booklet design and poster design, but wouldn\'92t ruin the structure or design if torn of.\
\
Lastly, i wanted to limit the use of ink in this project for two reasons. The less ink that is on the medium the easier the medium can be recycled, and that\'92s less consumption overall. Additionally because of the thickness of newsprint, I wanted to prevent any ghosting from the opposite side that\'92s not being viewed. To accomplish this I used only pure black and pure magenta as well as focused heavily on shape and gestalt for the type and graphic elements to create interest since I couldn\'92t rely on color or more saturated elements.\
\
Lastly, I wanted this project to be accessed, edited and produced easily anywhere. So if decided rather than using my first choice of instferstate sans serif typeface for this project I decided to design this project through Helvetica, with the expectation that it would also work with Arial or Arial Nova.\
\
This would mean that no matter where a person is and what machine they are using they can edit and produce this project to any quantity desired. }